        "heading": "Question and decision scope",
        "body": "Name the trigger that brought the case into the queue. Do not let a deadline expand a role beyond its written limit. Use the same definition across the queue and the review so the counts agree. In the I-9 re-verification timeliness queue, an item without a next action is treated as an exception. For the outsourced HR queue, the I-9 re-verification timeliness record is matched to the employee file and dated by the named HR owner before the case is closed.\n\nNote the observation window; a case without a period cannot be compared later. Distinguish a new request from a correction to an earlier one. Separate the request from the decision, because a complete request is not an approval. The I-9 re-verification timeliness reviewer records the reviewer name, the owner, and the next action in one place. The I-9 re-verification timeliness note stays in the HR service folder, where a second reviewer confirms it against the approved policy before the weekly audit.\n\nUse the same definition across the queue and the review so the counts agree. Say which system of record governs if two sources disagree. Keep the original request so a later correction does not erase the reason for the work. A sample I-9 re-verification timeliness review checks the I-9 record, the expiration date, and the surviving value in the HR record. At the monthly HR review, the I-9 re-verification timeliness item is re-read and any change is recorded in the same employee file."
